design discussion:

    this is a jitter buffer for RTP packets based on C++11 and STL.

    Memory management is something to be wary of; we will store our own
    pointer to given packets in our buffer and will return copies of these
    pointers when packets are popped off the buffer.  In the case of a
    buffer reset, or similar activity, we will go ahead and delete any
    packets in our buffer at the time.

    This implementation assumes that it is operating in a multi-threaded
    environment and syncronizes all operations/methods.

    Requirements:
        R0: eliminate effects of jitter in RTP packet arrival
        R1: detect and track missing packets in RTP stream
        R2: allow configuration of depth and packet size (in milliseconds)
        R3: track and report ongoing jitter stats:
                dropped packets
                out of order packets
                missed packets
                jitter (lifetime)
                max jitter (lifetime)
                current depth

    The RTPJitter will manage an internal buffer of RTP frames.  The application
    layer adds and removes packets from this buffer through the .push() and
    .pop() interface.  It is up to the application to manage and schedule this
    process on its own thread.

    A jitter buffer introduces a configured delay in the delivery and
    processing of packets; this is the "depth" in milliseconds of the buffer.
    Once the first packet is received into an empty buffer, the "depth" timer
    starts.  Once it expires, packets will be delivered upon request in the
    .pop() function until the buffer becomes empty again.

    The remaining logic involves how to handle newly arriving packets and
    handle the exception cases: missing or out-of-order packets.  ... and how
    to detect and address jitter.

    Jitter will be calculated as the mean deviation from the expected based on
    details, definitions, and sample code from RFC3550 section 6.4.1 and
    Appendix A.8.

    The jitter buffer will not examine, or differentiate, packets based on SSRC.


    note 1 - we use a std::deque to implement the internal buffer because it
        allows us to ignore the details of memory management for the buffer
        as it may grow and shrink.



RFC 4588 retransmission repair window
-------------------------------------

    In addition to the legacy depth/timer driven jitter buffer, RTPJitter can
    be switched into a repair mode that adds a fixed-frame retransmission
    (RTX) window.  Repair mode is deterministic: the caller supplies every
    clock reading as an int64 microsecond timestamp.  The buffer starts no
    threads, performs no sleeps and never reads a system clock.

    configuration

        RepairConfig holds primary_ssrc, rtx_ssrc, primary_pt, rtx_pt,
        clock_rate, packet_ms, delay_ms, retry_ms and window_packets.
        configure_repair() validates the whole structure first; on failure
        the existing configuration and buffered stream are left completely
        untouched.  On success the old stream is cleared and repair mode is
        entered.  reset() clears the stream and the timing anchor but keeps
        the repair configuration, so the same instance can be reused.

    call order

        1. configure_repair(cfg) once.  Before configuration push_at/pop_at
           return BAD_PACKET and nacks_at returns an empty vector.
        2. Feed every received datagram, primary or RTX, to
           push_at(packet, now_us).  The first legal primary packet anchors
           the stream at arrival A, sequence S and timestamp T.
        3. Call nacks_at(now_us) on your NACK timer and send the returned
           original media sequence numbers, in play order.
        4. Feed the resulting RTX packets back through push_at; each is
           restored and merged into the same slot map.
        5. Drive the player with pop_at(packet, now_us), one slot per call.
        6. Call reset() between streams; the repair configuration survives.

    repair scope and semantics

        - One slot map is used end to end.  Received retransmissions, issued
          NACKs and player dequeue all reference the original media sequence
          number; no separate retransmission queue is kept.  Slot k has
          sequence S+k (16-bit wrap), timestamp
          T + k*clock_rate*packet_ms/1000 (32-bit wrap) and playout instant
          A + delay_ms*1000 + k*packet_ms*1000, all integer arithmetic.
        - Only packets within window_packets of the current play head are
          accepted.  Too-old packets and timestamp mismatches return
          BAD_PACKET; packets beyond the window return BUFFER_OVERFLOW.
          Neither changes the buffer or any timing state.  A slot may still
          be filled exactly at its playout instant; one microsecond later it
          has expired.
        - The first content accepted for a slot wins.  Duplicate primary or
          RTX packets return SUCCESS without changing content, depth or
          clocks.
        - pop_at handles one known slot: BUFFERING before its deadline; at or
          after the deadline SUCCESS with the packet or DROPPED_PACKET for a
          gap, both advancing the head one slot; BUFFER_EMPTY past the newest
          known slot without advancing.  The output is rewritten only on
          SUCCESS.
        - nacks_at reports revealed, unfilled, unexpired gaps.  A gap is
          reported on the first query and again no sooner than retry_ms; the
          same instant never double-reports and missed rounds are not
          replayed.  Arriving repair cancels a gap immediately.  Queries
          neither advance playout nor move the anchor.
        - RTX packets carry the RFC 4588 two-byte big-endian OSN.  Outer
          SSRC/PT must match the RTX configuration; the outer sequence does
          not order anything.  CSRC and extension headers are traversed and
          padding validated.  On hand-off the OSN is removed and sequence,
          SSRC and PT are rewritten to primary values while marker,
          timestamp, CSRC, extension, media bytes and legal padding are
          preserved.  The caller's input object is never modified.

    While repair mode is active the legacy push()/pop() entry points return
    BAD_PACKET without touching state; outside repair mode they retain their
    original behaviour unchanged.

